# NEW (G9d), CONFIRMED: generic code changes meaning, unannounced in SPEC G9d. With T bound to 'mut Res&', '==' in a
# generic body compares two temporaries by identity (E10: a struct reference without Eq), so same(Res(1), Res(1))
# went from true to false; and a Hashable<T> constraint no longer holds (no Hash for a reference without Eq), so
# hashOf(Res(2)) is now refused.
# e27b83b: error[G19, E10b] 'Res& does not satisfy the constraint Hashable on T' at hashOf; with that line removed it
# prints 'false' (the evaluator agrees: a global 'G := same(Res(1), Res(1))' bakes as 'constant i1 false').
# 7345ff9: 'true' 'true' (and d2 d1 d1). b6259d1: 'true' 'true' (no destructor runs at all).
type Res struct(k I64) {
    k
} destruct { println("d" $k) }
fn same(a <T>, b T) Bool { return a == b }
fn hashOf(x <T Hashable<T>>) I64 { return x.Hash() }
fn main() ? {
    println($same(Res(1), Res(1)))
    println($(hashOf(Res(2)) != 0))
}
